  year={2026},
  volume={61},
  number={4},
  pages={1466-1476},
  abstract={This article presents a high-density (HD) 6T SRAM macro designed in 3-nm FinFET technology with an extended dual-rail (XDR) architecture, addressing active energy and leakage for mobile applications. Two key innovations are introduced: the delayed-wordline in write operation (DEWL) technique and a one-cycle latency low-leakage access mode (1-CLM). The XDR architecture enhances energy efficiency by operating most peripheral circuitry at a lower  $V_{\text {chip}}$  without dedicated level shifters (L/S). DEWL resolves write contention, achieving a 38% write-power reduction by reducing dummy read power and minimizing boost capacitance. The 1-CLM significantly improves standby leakage, demonstrating a 10% reduction by turning off bitline (BL) pre-chargers during no-operation (NOP) cycles, while ensuring rapid wake-up with minimal latency and peak current. Fabricated silicon results from a 563-kbit prototype show an impressive 36% reduction in average active energy, 3.89 pJ/access, compared to conventional designs. The macro achieves 35.5-Mbit/mm2 density, 27.5- $\mu $ W/Mbit standby leakage at  $25~^{\circ }$ C, and 6.94-pJ/access/Mbit total energy at  $85~^{\circ }$ C, demonstrating a solution for energy-efficient and reliable SRAMs in aggressively scaled technologies.},
