  abstract={This article introduces an 11.4-to-16.4-GHz digital phase-locked loop (PLL) for chirp generation in frequency-modulated continuous-wave (FMCW) radars. The PLL is implemented in a frequency-domain, type-II two-point modulation (TPM) structure. A cycle-slip compensation scheme is proposed to identify overflow events in the time-to-digital converter (TDC) and correct the miscalculated frequency error, thereby improving chirp linearity during fast chirp generation. To reduce frequency errors caused by frequency overlaps in a multi-bank digitally controlled oscillator (DCO), a back-tracking digital pre-distortion (DPD) scheme is introduced to ensure robust convergence despite capacitor mismatch-induced asymmetric frequency overlaps in triangular chirp modulations. The FMCW digital PLL, implemented in a 28-nm CMOS technology, consumes 10 mW of power and occupies an area of 0.19 mm2. It is capable of generating a frequency modulation (FM) chirp with a 960-MHz/ $\mu $ s slope and a 3.84-GHz bandwidth while achieving a normalized rms FM error of 0.032%.},
